WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.487 --> 00:00:09.980
[NON-ENGLISH SINGING]

00:00:11.976 --> 00:00:15.469
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:32.990
The Music and Theater Arts--

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.590
they inspire all of MIT with
new ideas, new experiences,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.010
and just makes them better
people, and better scientists,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.270
better engineers--

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:42.980
just better communicators.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:44.690
What's really exciting
about MIT is it

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:47.550
has this unique opportunity
for people like me,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.550
who are passionate about
so many different types

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.790
of things, where I don't
have to be a music major

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.040
to participate in
high level music.

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.020
Once we start
rehearsal, I completely

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.230
forget that I have
a PSET due tomorrow,

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.660
or that I have an
exam in three days.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.718
It's just an experience that
completely takes you away.

00:01:04.718 --> 00:01:06.635
And I think that's what
makes it so enjoyable.

00:01:06.635 --> 00:01:10.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.860
They sound amazing, and it made
me forget that they are not

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.150
majoring in, necessarily--

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.620
[LAUGHING]

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.620
--music.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.790
So that's pretty amazing, the
high level that they have here.

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.210
They're working
very hard in school.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.810
To take time for music is
not easy for many of them,

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.410
and yet they bring
their best game to it.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:34.720
And that makes it a
pleasure to work on things.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.200
94-- winds who have
these 16th note

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.300
figures, those should
not be articulate.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.690
Through arts at MIT, I've been
able to interact with literally

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.580
my heroes, musically--

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:52.780
Jamshied Sharifi, Chick Corea,
Anat Cohen, Miguel Zenón,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:53.380
Don Byron.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:58.570
(SINGING) We're gonna
have a funky good time.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:00.400
These students are
so interested in life

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.270
and trying so hard to
figure out what they can do,

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:05.890
and discovering skills.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.780
It's great to be part of that.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.150
It gives me energy every year.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.170
There are concerts here that
I would never have imagined

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.810
being able to be a part of--

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.470
the Jacob Collier
experience, going

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.950
to Puerto Rico with Miguel,
the Clarinet Summit.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.373
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.620
So this is typical Fred.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.380
I was just walking down the
Infinite Corridor one day,

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:33.880
and I just happened
to run into him.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:37.780
And he says, oh, hey, Eran, I
have this idea that-- and what

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.530
are you doing on May 11?

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.560
And I look at my calendar, and
I said, yeah, I think I'm free.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.710
He's like, great,
because we're going

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:46.840
to do this Clarinet Summit.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:47.887
It's going to be amazing.

00:02:47.887 --> 00:02:48.720
I hope you can play.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.010
I was like, OK,
sounds good, thanks.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.900
This whole thing came
to me in a dream,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.390
and I can't believe
it actually happened.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.030
It hasn't happened yet,
but we've gotten this far.

00:02:58.030 --> 00:02:59.950
So this is very, very cool.

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:03.072
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.300
There's been more and more of
a commitment throughout MIT

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:10.600
to support artistic endeavors,
even the wildest and most

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.130
ambitious ones.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.830
And there's been
people like Fred,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:18.430
who are willing to be
ambitious, to come up

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.250
with a wild and amazing idea
that then comes to life.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.666
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.930
Fred is MIT's Energizer Bunny.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.720
He works harder than all of us.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.790
Fred, if you're watching
this, go to bed.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.630
Seriously, he should sleep more.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:47.250
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.110
OK, good.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.138
I have a rehearsal pedagogy that
I'm going to suggest for you.

00:03:52.138 --> 00:03:54.680
And then I'm going to leave,
and then I'm going to come back.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:56.580
Fred really is a force
to be reckoned with.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.590
He doesn't stop, he keeps going.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.030
And his brain is always active.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.090
And he's got so many ideas,
and he's just brilliant.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.920
And he loves people,
and he loves music.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.330
And he is a great
collaborator too.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.470
I think I maybe went
too far the other way.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:12.900
So I have to bring
it back, but--

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.858
It's better than the ink,
that's all I can say.

00:04:14.858 --> 00:04:18.189
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.240
The man has drive, and
he is so incredible

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.440
to watch and work with.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.690
And the passion he
exudes is amazing.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:31.340
Is there time?

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.830
Could we have one
hour on Friday?

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:35.810
Do you have time?

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.102
See if you could figure it out.

00:04:37.102 --> 00:04:39.080
If you can't, we might
not need a whole hour.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.630
But I think if we could run
the piece a thousand times

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.400
Friday, that would be good.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.010
He's respectful
of the past, but I

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:49.700
think he's got a
vision for the future,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.430
for himself and for the program.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:56.570
And I think he works tirelessly
towards those visions.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.690
The students who have had
the chance to work with Fred,

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:03.980
and FJE, and MITWE, are working
with the musical presence

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.790
that's as powerful as Herb was.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:09.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.610
It's profound.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.360
I know it was for me
and still is for me.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.307
And I think it will be
for all these students,

00:05:17.307 --> 00:05:19.640
regardless of the paths they
take, whether it's in music

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:20.140
or not.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.216
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:28.870
We hired Fred from his previous
job, which was as a high school

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.310
wind leader.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:33.927
We thought he was just the
most top flight candidate

00:05:33.927 --> 00:05:34.885
we could have imagined.

00:05:34.885 --> 00:05:37.966
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.730
He's been willing to
take chances on things

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.880
that seem crazy at the outset.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.550
Getting Jacob to come for
a week, I was like, Fred,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:52.515
you're not going to
get that to happen.

00:05:52.515 --> 00:05:53.640
And now he's done it twice.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.979
[VOCALIZING]

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.780
Just watching him
conduct on stage,

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.810
you think, wow, I want to have
that level of appreciation

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.630
for music, for such a
breadth of music too.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:11.830
He does so many interesting
things, and he loves all of it

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:12.330
dearly.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.823
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.260
He's definitely someone who's
very humanitarian oriented

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.150
and really wants to
make a social impact

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:24.530
and bring the
community together.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.990
Fred's guests have
been a diverse crowd.

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:48.610
And his idea of where he
would like to be involved

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.560
has really pushed
in the direction

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.185
of understanding other
cultures that are marginalized.

00:06:53.185 --> 00:06:56.248
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:00.780
When the hurricane first hit,
he helped organize a fundraiser

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.880
with MIT Wind Ensemble
and the different groups

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.980
he does to send
supplies to Puerto Rico.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.150
The whale song was one
of Fred's wacky ideas.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:13.860
So the idea is that maybe this
baby whale, or mother and baby,

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.110
are caught in the net,
and it's horribleness.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.060
And then this is sort
of a requiem for them.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.511
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.120
When Fred first
introduced the idea

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:32.200
of playing with whale sounds,
I was kind of confused.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.840
And when we performed it
in front of the audience,

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.355
you could tell that
they were feeling

00:07:37.355 --> 00:07:38.605
what we were trying to convey.

00:07:38.605 --> 00:07:41.914
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.660
Fred's a good drummer, and
I've played with him a lot.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.310
The kids in the band get a
big kick out of the times

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:01.900
when he plays because they
don't get to actually hear him

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:02.587
very often.

00:08:02.587 --> 00:08:05.996
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.005
He knows how to lift musicians
and make them want to shine.

00:08:17.005 --> 00:08:19.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.050
He just has such a warm,
inspiring character.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.000
And so he knows how
to make people relax,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.940
and he knows how to push people,
for them to do their best.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.760
I'd like you to emote more.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.039
I'd like you to-- for
lack of a better phrase--

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:35.010
sell it musically
more, by exaggerating

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:38.669
the emotional moments, via
dynamics and your soul.

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.890
I started out as
one of his mentors

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.720
when he was 14 years old.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:45.630
I was a big deal to him.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.510
And I told the MIT Wind
Ensemble the other night

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:50.700
when I rehearsed with
them, at this point

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.770
he's one of my mentors.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:53.940
He's a big deal to me.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:57.090 --> 00:09:00.000
Fred single-handedly got
me through undergrad,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.860
either indirectly
or directly, by just

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.680
encouraging me to make music.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.047
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.040
Every time I talk to him, I
get so much joy because he

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.200
genuinely cares
about his students,

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:15.820
and he really works
hard to make sure

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.935
that we're also just doing
well as human beings outside

00:09:18.935 --> 00:09:19.435
of music.

00:09:19.435 --> 00:09:22.606
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.802
Fred is also very
mindful of challenges

00:09:27.802 --> 00:09:29.260
that other people
are dealing with.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.510
And he would check
in on me all the time

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:33.440
and say, how can I help?

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.120
What can I do?

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.900
And honestly, having his support
when I was going through a lot

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:41.862
was incredible.

00:09:41.862 --> 00:09:43.570
And I know I'm not
the only student who's

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:44.827
had that experience with Fred.

00:09:44.827 --> 00:09:46.660
So I've had lots of
teachers over the years,

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:47.960
I've worked with lots
of different people.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.200
And I do have to say that
Fred Harris is perhaps

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.690
the most inspirational
person I've ever worked with.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:57.130
A great human being,
a great family man,

00:09:57.130 --> 00:10:00.760
a great colleague to all of
us in the music education

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:04.210
community, and a
compassionate and caring

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:08.260
teacher for, now, thousands
and thousands of students.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:12.130
He's very genuine about
what he's expressing.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.110
I think Fred is a genius.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:20.405
Sensitive, but also
extremely smart and witty.

00:10:20.405 --> 00:10:22.030
The unique thing
about a Fred rehearsal

00:10:22.030 --> 00:10:26.380
is how much time they spend
actually playing the music

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.510
and how little time he
expends explaining it.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:31.600
Just a great mentor.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:35.058
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:52.348 --> 00:10:53.630
How is that water phone?

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:54.220
I heard it.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:54.580
Yeah, I like it.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.240
It's pretty cool, right?

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:55.740
Yeah.

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:57.610
Yeah, I'll double
check, but I think so.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:57.880
OK.

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:58.838
Thank you for checking.

00:10:58.838 --> 00:11:01.120
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:02.010
Good night.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:02.910
[LAUGHING]

00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

